# Module Conventions

One feature = one module folder, self-contained, one nav item. This file is the baseline every
Customapp portal follows regardless of its data layer (OASIS/SQL Server, Supabase, or a direct
Lambda API) — see "Data-layer bindings" at the bottom for the parts that differ by stack.

## Project Structure

- Modules live at `modules/{module_name}/` (repo root, siblings of `js/`, `css/`, not nested under
  an app-specific tree).
- Each module has `html/`, `js/`, and (optional) `css/` subdirectories.
- Naming convention: `{module_name}_{type}.{ext}` (e.g. `employees_grid.html`, `employees_grid.js`).
- Prefer separate grid and form files/routes over one combined view.

```
modules/{name}/html/{name}_grid.html
modules/{name}/js/{name}_grid.js
modules/{name}/css/{name}_grid.css   (optional)
```

## Module Types

- Grid view: `{module}_grid.html`, `{module}_grid.js`, `{module}_grid.css`
- Form view: `{module}_form.html`, `{module}_form.js`, `{module}_form.css`

## JavaScript Patterns

- Routed module object (IIFE): `var _{module}Grid = function () { return { init, initHandlers,
  loadGrid, ... } }();` — invoked by the app router when its route loads.
- Standalone module (object literal, self-loaded HTML): `var {module}Grid = { init: async
  function() { ... } };` initialized with `$(document).ready(function() { {module}Grid.init(); });`
- Use event delegation with `.off('.namespace').on('.namespace', ...)` so re-initialization never
  double-binds handlers.
- Must include `_{module}Grid.init();` / `_{module}Form.init();` (or the standalone equivalent) at
  the bottom of the JS file.
- Always include breadcrumb / page-context navigation appropriate to the shell in use.

## Edit Mode Detection Pattern

- Store the record GUID on module scope at init (e.g. `scope.current{Module}GUID`); do not rely
  only on route params for reliability across reloads.
- Edit detection: `const isEdit = scope.current{Module}GUID && scope.current{Module}GUID !== '';`
- Click-to-read: persist the selected row (stored GUID / `window._selected{Module}Data`) then
  navigate to the form route; show a loading state during the fetch.

## Delete Functionality

- Confirm destructive actions with a modal/dialog before calling delete (SweetAlert2 where the
  stack already uses it: `Swal.fire({ title, text, icon: 'warning', showCancelButton: true, ... })`).
- Show a success/error toast and refresh the grid after a successful delete.

## Error Handling & Loading States

- Every async operation shows a loading state and handles both success and failure.
- Surface failures to the user via toast/notification, not just a console log.
- A "no confident match" fallback (search, lookup, classifier) must never silently pick a value
  for a required field — leave it blank so the user makes an explicit choice. A silent wrong guess
  is worse than a visibly empty field.
- Never fire one backend call per grid/list row on load — it's invisible with a handful of dev
  rows but fans out to hundreds of concurrent calls against real data and can saturate a shared
  connection pool for other users. Load per-row state via one batch call keyed by row ids, and
  **fail open** (don't block the feature) if the batch check itself errors.
- When a default/lookup value is duplicated in both a client-side constant and a server-side seed,
  update both together and backfill existing records — otherwise one falls silently out of sync.

## Validation Patterns

- Required fields: visual indicator (e.g. `*`) plus validation styling (e.g. `.is-invalid`).
- Validate on the client for UX; the authoritative validation is always server-side.
- South African field rules (where applicable to the client base):
  - ID number: 13 digits; gender digit 7–10 ≥ 5000 = Male, < 5000 = Female; century from the YY
    prefix (> 50 → 1900s, ≤ 50 → 2000s).
  - Mobile number: `^(\+27|0)[0-9]{9}$`
  - Email: use the shared validator already in the project rather than re-implementing regex.

## Security

- Escape/sanitize any untrusted content before rendering it — use text-insertion APIs (`.text()`,
  `textContent`) for user-supplied strings; never `.html()`/`innerHTML`/`document.write()` with
  untrusted input. See `javascript-jquery-rules.md` for the full security ruleset.

## Navigation Integration

- Add the nav item in the shell (root HTML / nav partial) and gate its visibility from the shell's
  nav script based on the user's role/permissions — the exact function name is app-specific, but
  every module must be reachable only through a gated nav entry, never hardcoded visible-by-default.
- Register the module's routes wherever this project's router config lives (see the data-layer
  binding for your stack below for the concrete file).

## When Creating a Module

1. Generate the grid/form files following the naming convention above.
2. Implement full CRUD with field names matching the actual schema (not assumed defaults).
3. Wire routing and gated navigation.
4. Implement error handling, loading states, and required-field validation.
5. Apply South African field rules where the module collects ID/mobile/email data.
6. Use stored-GUID edit-mode detection and click-to-read.
7. Confirm deletes before executing them.
8. Follow the data-layer binding for this project's stack (below) for the actual service-call shape.

